- Story & Context: Named after the phantom arctic territory of old maps, this track was written during Vance’s brief exile in a remote lighthouse following an argument over the group's production budget. Using only a portable DAT recorder, a single kettledrum, and a pocket synthesizer, Vance captured the sound of wind whistling through brass window frames and layered it against a rolling 134 BPM disco bassline. The song features a tearful, spoken-word vocal from a maritime radio operator who refused to abandon her post during the storm of November 1996. Her voice wavered over the soaring trance pads as she whispered into the mic, "Coordinates lost, but I still feel the rhythm of your coast."
